From: Ahrion Gallegos <ahrionmgallegos@gmail.com>
Subject: net: wwan: mhi_wwan_mbim: deliver RX datagrams through gro_cells

The MBIM RX path hands every de-aggregated datagram to the stack with
netif_rx(): no NAPI context, no GRO. On an RM520N-GL the MHI controller
sets a 32KB MRU (mhi_quectel_rm5xx_info.mru_default), so one MHI
transfer carries an NTB of up to ~21 full-size datagrams — each of which
then walks conntrack/netfilter/IP individually. At 5G downlink rates on
a dual Cortex-A53 router that per-packet cost is the WAN bottleneck.

Switch delivery to gro_cells (net/core/gro_cells.c), the stock mechanism
for exactly this class of callback-driven virtual driver (used by
ip_tunnel, vxlan, ...): per-CPU NAPI contexts drain a small queue via
napi_gro_receive(), giving the MBIM path real NAPI instances, GRO
batching of the ~21-datagram bursts, and (as a side effect) a working
/sys/class/net/<dev>/threaded toggle. The MHI DL callback runs in
tasklet (BH) context — same context tunnels call gro_cells_receive()
from.

Details:
  * per-link gro_cells in struct mhi_mbim_link, created in ndo_init and
    destroyed in ndo_uninit. That ties them to exactly the netdev's
    registered lifetime and lets the core do the unwinding:
    register_netdevice() calls ndo_uninit on its own failure path, and
    unregister_netdevice() calls it after the RCU grace period, once
    nothing can still reach the cells. This is what geneve does, and what
    vxlan was moved to by ad6c9986bcb6 ("vxlan: Fix GRO cells race
    condition between receive and link delete"); bareudp, macsec and
    ip_tunnel pair the same way. Between hlist_add_head_rcu() and
    ndo_init the cells are not allocated yet, which gro_cells_receive()
    already handles — it falls back to netif_rx() when !gcells->cells.
  * newlink additionally un-hashes the link when register_netdevice()
    fails. Upstream left it hashed on that error path, so RX could still
    find a half-dead link; dellink is otherwise untouched by this patch.
  * a cheap 4-tuple hash is set on each datagram before hand-off, purely
    so GRO can bucket. dev_gro_receive() indexes napi->gro_hash[] with
    skb_get_hash_raw() and gro_list_prepare() uses the same value as a
    fast reject; netdev_alloc_skb() leaves it 0, so every flow shares
    bucket 0 of GRO_HASH_BUCKETS(8) and MAX_GRO_SKBS(8) then caps the
    device at eight flows held mid-aggregation instead of sixty-four,
    with the cheap reject never firing. Tagged PKT_HASH_TYPE_NONE, so
    l4_hash/sw_hash stay clear and skb_get_hash() still computes a
    canonical hash for anything needing real flow isolation (fq_codel,
    RPS) -- this value never leaves GRO's fast path. A full flow
    dissection here would cost more than it saves: GRO merges N datagrams
    into fewer skbs before the qdisc ever asks for a hash, so it would
    pay N dissections to avoid M, with M < N.
  * NETIF_F_GRO is advertised and set: gro_cells_receive() falls back
    to netif_rx() when the feature bit is off, so `ethtool -K <dev>
    gro off` is a live kill-switch back to today's behaviour.
  * mac/network headers are anchored at skb->data before hand-off.
    netdev_alloc_skb() leaves mac_header at the ~0U "unset" sentinel.
    netif_rx() tolerated that, but gro_cells feeds napi_gro_receive(),
    and consumers that do mac_header-relative arithmetic (generic XDP's
    unguarded __skb_push() in do_xdp_generic(), packet taps, GSO
    helpers) then compute skb->data - skb_mac_header(skb) against the
    sentinel, underflow, and walk before skb->head. Anchoring makes
    mac_len 0 and that difference 0. rmnet_deliver_skb() does the same;
    cf. upstream fixes qmi_wwan "Add missing skb_reset_mac_header-call"
    (skb_under_panic on a raw-IP wwan0) and 6lowpan "reset mac_header
    after decompress to fix panic".
  * TX path, MBIM framing, MRU handling: untouched.

Out-of-tree patch for the GL-X3000 lean build; bench before trusting
(iperf3 downlink CPU + latency-under-load A/B, gro on vs off).

diff -uprN a/drivers/net/wwan/Kconfig b/drivers/net/wwan/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/net/wwan/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/net/wwan/Kconfig
@@ -52,6 +52,7 @@ config MHI_WWAN_CTRL
 config MHI_WWAN_MBIM
         tristate "MHI WWAN MBIM network driver for QCOM-based PCIe modems"
         depends on MHI_BUS
+        select GRO_CELLS
         help
           MHI WWAN MBIM is a WWAN network driver for QCOM-based PCIe modems.
           It implements MBIM over MHI, for IP data aggregation and muxing.
diff -uprN a/drivers/net/wwan/mhi_wwan_mbim.c b/drivers/net/wwan/mhi_wwan_mbim.c
